class_name LevelData
extends RefCounted
##
## LEVEL DATA - every arena in the game, as plain data.
##
## There are no room scenes to click around in. Each arena is a dictionary and
## `room.gd` builds the geometry from it. That keeps the whole level design in
## ONE readable file, which is much easier to explain in a video (and much
## harder to break than five hand-edited .tscn files).
##
## Coordinate system: the camera-free playfield is exactly 1280x720 with a
## 26px wall all the way around. Interior obstacles are listed per arena; the
## border is generated by `border_walls()`.
##
## FOUR ROUNDS OF THE GRAND MELEE
##   The arenas are not four variations of one box. Each is a different piece
##   of architecture, and each one teaches exactly one idea from the colour
##   wheel. Architecture is doing work here: it decides how far a knight can
##   see, where paint can pool, and where the player has to commit.
##
##     ARENA 01  THE OPEN SAND     a low walled ring with four gates, a wide
##                                 concourse around it, and two herald plinths.
##                                 Nothing to hide in but your own paint: the
##                                 lesson is MATCHING (simultaneous contrast).
##     ARENA 02  THE BANNERED HALL a cross of four halls meeting at one open
##                                 crossing, with rooms in the quadrants and a
##                                 lane around the outside. RED walks the east-
##                                 west hall, BLUE the north-south one, and the
##                                 only place their paint can meet is the
##                                 crossing: the lesson is MIXING.
##     ARENA 03  THE DEEP RING     a ring wall with four gates around a sunken
##                                 pit holding two altars. The floor is small and
##                                 every route crosses every other route: the
##                                 lesson is COMPLEMENTS, and hiding does not
##                                 save you here.
##     ARENA 04  THE WHEEL         eight pillars around a central dais, and a
##                                 Champion whose hue circles the wheel. He
##                                 fights in three phases and calls Aspirants.
##
## THE KNIGHT ARCHETYPES (spec section 4, as plain data - no new classes)
##     ASPIRANT  hp  60  damage 12  speed  75  the basic sword
##     WARDEN    hp 140  damage 25  speed  56  slow, heavy, knocks you back
##     HARRIER   hp  45  damage 10  speed 118  fast, fragile, rushes you
##   Every one of them is a `guards` entry with different hp / speed / damage.
##
## THE COLOUR WHEEL.
## The six knight colours are exact HSV hues, written out as RGB because a
## GDScript const cannot call Color.from_hsv(). The hue of each one is in the
## comment beside it, and the game's own test suite re-derives every hue from
## the RGB values to prove the numbers below are what they claim to be.
##
##     RED     (1.00, 0.00, 0.00)   =   0 deg
##     ORANGE  (1.00, 0.50, 0.00)   =  30 deg
##     YELLOW  (1.00, 1.00, 0.00)   =  60 deg
##     GREEN   (0.00, 1.00, 0.00)   = 120 deg
##     BLUE    (0.00, 0.00, 1.00)   = 240 deg
##     PURPLE  (0.75, 0.00, 1.00)   = 285 deg
##
## COMPLEMENTARY PAIRS THAT ACTUALLY EXIST IN THE GAME (tolerance 25 deg):
##     YELLOW 60  <-> BLUE 240     gap 180, off by  0  -> YES
##     GREEN 120  <-> PURPLE 285   gap 165, off by 15  -> YES
##     ORANGE 30  <-> BLUE 240     gap 150, off by 30  -> NO  (deliberately)
##     RED 0      <-> nothing                          -> NO  (deliberately)
##
## Those last two lines are the lesson: "opposite on the wheel" is a real,
## measurable relationship, not a vibe. The whole combo system is built on it.
##
## WAVES
##   Each arena feeds its knights in packs. A `"waves"` count plus a `"wave"`
##   tag per knight decide when a knight steps onto the sand; a spec with no tag
##   is wave 1, and an arena with no wave data fights its whole cast at once.
##   Two of the packs in every wave room bring a real complementary pair, so the
##   combo is always available inside a single 3-second window.

const PLAYFIELD: Vector2 = Vector2(1280.0, 720.0)
const WALL: float = 26.0

## Default 2D radius of a knight's body and of the player. Kept here so the
## level-validity test can check that paths never clip a wall.
const GUARD_RADIUS: float = 14.0
const BOSS_RADIUS: float = 34.0
const PLAYER_RADIUS: float = 12.0

## Seconds at the start of an arena during which guards cannot fill their
## detection meter. Without this, spawning inside a patrol cone feels unfair.
const SPAWN_GRACE: float = 1.4


## The four Rect2s that make the outer wall of the playfield.
static func border_walls() -> Array:
	var w: float = WALL
	var p: Vector2 = PLAYFIELD
	return [
		Rect2(0.0, 0.0, p.x, w),                 # top
		Rect2(0.0, p.y - w, p.x, w),             # bottom
		Rect2(0.0, w, w, p.y - w * 2.0),         # left
		Rect2(p.x - w, w, w, p.y - w * 2.0),     # right
	]


## The one source of truth for "where are the walls in this arena".
## Both the game (room.gd) and the test suite call this, so a level can never
## be tested against different geometry from the geometry it actually runs with
## - which is the classic way a "passing" level test becomes meaningless.
static func walls_for(room: Dictionary) -> Array:
	var walls: Array = border_walls()
	for extra in room.get("walls", []):
		walls.append(extra)
	return walls


## Every arena in play order. Index 3 is the Champion's arena.
